---
// src/components/SeoGuideContent.astro
---
<section id="guide" class="max-w-4xl mx-auto space-y-12 text-[var(--foreground)] leading-relaxed">
  
  <!-- Section Eyebrow -->
  <div class="text-center space-y-3">
    <span class="text-xs uppercase font-bold tracking-widest text-cyan-400 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-cyan-500/10 border border-cyan-500/20">
      Authoritative 2026 Web Engineering Guide
    </span>
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight">
      The Modern Favicon Size & Format Specification
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      Cut through obsolete 2012 tutorials. Learn how modern Chromium, WebKit, and Gecko engines handle vector SVGs, multi-resolution ICO containers, and mobile PWA manifests today.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Content Block 1: Why Most Favicon Guides Are 10 Years Outdated -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--card)] p-8 shadow-lg space-y-5">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--foreground)] flex items-center gap-2.5">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-indigo-500/15 text-indigo-400 font-mono text-sm flex items-center justify-center font-black">
        01
      </span>
      Why 90% of Favicon Size Guides Are Ten Years Outdated
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      If you search the web for favicon size recommendations, you will find dozens of tutorials telling you to export over 20 separate PNG files—including obsolete sizes like <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-[var(--muted)] font-mono text-xs">mstile-144x144.png</code> for Windows 8 Metro tiles, legacy <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-[var(--muted)] font-mono text-xs">apple-touch-icon-57x57.png</code> for the iPhone 3GS, and complex XML configurations.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      In <strong>2026</strong>, modern operating systems and browser engines (Chromium 80+, Safari 16+, Firefox Quantum, Edge) have standardized their asset resolution algorithms. Modern devices automatically downscale high-resolution assets using hardware-accelerated bicubic interpolation. Supplying 20+ separate link tags bloats your initial HTML <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-[var(--muted)] font-mono text-xs">&lt;head&gt;</code> payload, triggers unnecessary speculative HTTP requests, and increases maintenance overhead.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Content Block 2: The Modern 3-File Golden Rule -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--card)] p-8 shadow-lg space-y-5">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--foreground)] flex items-center gap-2.5">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-cyan-500/15 text-cyan-400 font-mono text-sm flex items-center justify-center font-black">
        02
      </span>
      The Modern 3-File Golden Rule for Maximum Performance
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      Web performance experts and W3C engineers recommend the <strong>Streamlined 3-File Favicon Stack</strong>. This lightweight setup covers <strong>99.8% of global devices</strong> with zero visual degradation:
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-2">
      <!-- File 1: SVG -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-purple-500/30 bg-purple-500/5 space-y-2">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
          <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-purple-400">1. favicon.svg</span>
          <span class="text-[10px] px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-purple-500/20 text-purple-300 font-semibold">Universal</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
          Infinitely scalable vector format. Renders razor-sharp on 1080p, 4K, 5K, and Retina displays while weighing under 3 KB. Supports native CSS dark/light theme switching.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- File 2: ICO -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-cyan-500/30 bg-cyan-500/5 space-y-2">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
          <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-cyan-400">2. favicon.ico</span>
          <span class="text-[10px] px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-cyan-500/20 text-cyan-300 font-semibold">Fallback</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
          Packs both 16×16 and 32×32 bitmap layers. Placed directly at the root URL <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">/favicon.ico</code> to satisfy automated crawlers, RSS readers, and legacy desktop browsers.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- File 3: Apple Touch -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-indigo-500/30 bg-indigo-500/5 space-y-2">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
          <span class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-indigo-400">3. apple-touch-icon</span>
          <span class="text-[10px] px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-indigo-500/20 text-indigo-300 font-semibold">180×180 px</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
          High-density square PNG for iOS and iPadOS "Add to Home Screen" bookmarks. Apple automatically rounds the corners with its proprietary squircle curve.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Content Block 3: Dynamic SVG Favicons with Dark Mode -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--card)] p-8 shadow-lg space-y-5">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--foreground)] flex items-center gap-2.5">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/15 text-emerald-400 font-mono text-sm flex items-center justify-center font-black">
        03
      </span>
      Dynamic SVG Favicons with Native Dark Mode Switching
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      A common frustration for web designers is an icon that looks great on a light browser tab, but becomes completely invisible when a user switches to dark mode. Because SVG files can embed CSS stylesheets, you can embed a <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-[var(--muted)] font-mono text-xs">@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)</code> query directly inside your vector favicon:
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-800 bg-[#0f141c] p-4 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto">
      <pre><code>&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 100 100"&gt;
  &lt;style&gt;
    path &#123; fill: #1e293b; &#125;
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) &#123;
      path &#123; fill: #38bdf8; &#125;
    &#125;
  &lt;/style&gt;
  &lt;path d="..." /&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-[var(--muted-foreground)]">
      When a user toggles their OS or browser between Light and Dark mode, the browser instantly updates the tab favicon fill color without requiring any JavaScript or page reload.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Content Block 4: The Google SERP Favicon Rule (48x48 Multiples) -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--card)] p-8 shadow-lg space-y-5">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--foreground)] flex items-center gap-2.5">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/15 text-amber-400 font-mono text-sm flex items-center justify-center font-black">
        04
      </span>
      The Google Search SERP Favicon Rule: Multiples of 48px Square
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      Google displays website favicons prominently beside search results on both mobile and desktop SERP pages. Having a crisp, recognizable favicon significantly improves organic Click-Through Rates (CTR).
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      However, Google Search Central documentation specifies a strict requirement: <strong>your favicon image must be a multiple of 48px square</strong> (e.g. 48×48, 96×96, 144×144, or 192×192 px). If your favicon is non-square (such as 16×32) or is smaller than 48px, Google may refuse to index your icon and instead display a generic gray globe placeholder.
    </p>
    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/10 text-xs text-amber-200">
      <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Our generator automatically produces <code class="font-mono">favicon-48x48.png</code> and <code class="font-mono">favicon-96x96.png</code> to ensure instant recognition and compliance with Googlebot crawler requirements.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Content Block 5: PWA Web App Manifest Configuration -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--card)] p-8 shadow-lg space-y-5">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--foreground)] flex items-center gap-2.5">
      <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-teal-500/15 text-teal-400 font-mono text-sm flex items-center justify-center font-black">
        05
      </span>
      Progressive Web App (PWA) Manifest: Maskable vs Any Icons
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      To make a website installable on modern Android and desktop Chrome, you must provide a <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-[var(--muted)] font-mono text-xs">site.webmanifest</code> file declaring two primary icon resolutions: <strong>192×192 px</strong> (home screen launch) and <strong>512×512 px</strong> (splash screen).
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted-foreground)] leading-relaxed">
      Modern Android devices use adaptive icon shaping (circles, rounded squares, squircle masks). If you do not configure the <code class="font-mono text-xs">"purpose": "maskable"</code> property with appropriate 10% safe-zone padding, Android will place your icon inside an unsightly white rounded rectangle with thick borders. Favvicon.com generates both <code class="font-mono text-xs">"any"</code> and <code class="font-mono text-xs">"maskable"</code> entries automatically.
    </p>
  </div>

</section>
